{:up ["ALTER TABLE recipe_history ADD COLUMN source TEXT"
      "ALTER TABLE recipes ADD COLUMN source TEXT"]
 ;; Where one *version* came from: `'ui'` or `'machine'`, the owner's own two
 ;; words for the distinction — and the only one cookbook can authenticate,
 ;; since a machine token is checkable where a claim about a browser is not.
 ;; Two columns because the current version lives on the row and the superseded
 ;; ones live on their history rows, which is the same split `version` itself
 ;; already has: history holds 1..N-1 and the row is N.
 ;;
 ;; **Nullable with no default, and NULL is a third category — 'unrecorded' —
 ;; not a synonym for `'machine'`.** Every row and every history row that exists
 ;; when this runs comes out NULL, and nothing can recover what wrote them:
 ;; `recipe_history` never had an author column, and `recipes.user_id` cannot
 ;; answer it either, because a machine token deliberately acts in the *owner's*
 ;; scope, so its rows carry the owner's id (see `login-handler`). A `DEFAULT
 ;; 'machine'` would therefore be a silent claim about every version the owner
 ;; wrote by hand before anybody was counting, and the mirror-image claim is no
 ;; better. Unrecorded provenance is not machine provenance, the same way 004
 ;; held that unknown provenance is not human provenance — so the UI is given a
 ;; third bucket to show rather than a lie to round to.
 ;;
 ;; This is *per-version* authorship, which is exactly what 004 said it was not
 ;; answering: its `has_human_edit` is one monotonic bit about the row. The bit
 ;; stays — it is what `?human=true` reads, and a filter that had to aggregate
 ;; over history on every listing read is the thing the house avoided when it put
 ;; the version number on the row. Going forward the bit is derivable from these
 ;; columns (true exactly when some version reads `'ui'`), so the same write sets
 ;; both and they cannot disagree.
 :down ["ALTER TABLE recipes DROP COLUMN source"
        "ALTER TABLE recipe_history DROP COLUMN source"]}
